// CALLER CENSUS FOR THE RENAME, recorded because a rename that moves a symbol's MEANING is the
// section 3 meaning-fork, and "I checked" is not a fact a later reader can verify. The row formerly
// named cax41_catalog became cax41_catalog_superseded and a NEW row took the old name, so every
// consumer of that name now reads a different tariff. Three consumers existed at the rename and all
// three are accounted for:
//   test.claim.hetzner_cost_quote_witness - updated in the same change; its pinned literal moved
//     50500 -> 65700 deliberately, and it now ALSO asserts the superseded row at 50500, so a silent
//     swap of one row for the other reds rather than passing.
//   test.claim.hetzner_listing_witness - authored in the same change; asserts both rows by identity.
//   strategy.node_rental (private tree) - imported the name but had already stopped reading it;
//     the dead import and the prose naming it were removed in the same landing.
// No consumer silently changed meaning. A future rename here owes the same census.

data cax41_catalog_usd: HetznerCloudServerCatalogRow = HetznerCloudServerCatalogRow {
  server_type: Cax41,
  vcpus: hardware_thread_count(16),
  memory_bytes: byte_size(34359738368),
  disk_bytes: byte_size(343597383680),
  hourly: money_amount_micro(count: 77700),
  monthly_cap: money_amount_micro(count: 48490000),
  currency: Usd,
  tariff: TariffEffectiveFrom {
    date: "2026-06-15 08:00 CEST",
    citation: "new-price column, price-adjustment document GE-D9256; agrees with the signed-in console reading of 2026-09-05",
  },
  pricing_citation: "GE-D9256 (new-price column, USD)",
  cpu_architecture: Aarch64,
}

// THE TWO CURRENCIES ARE FX-CONSISTENT, which is the finding that retired a claim this repository
// briefly held. 40.99 EUR at the ECB reference rate of 1.1622 is about 47.64 USD against a listed
// 48.49 - under two per cent apart, and the superseded pair is consistent to the same order. So
// this vendor does NOT charge a currency premium, and a model may not refuse FX valuation on the
// grounds that it would. What it must refuse is labelling an FX-derived figure as the vendor's
// quote in that currency, and joining two tariff VERSIONS across two currencies - which is the
// arithmetic that manufactured the premium in the first place.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE CLOUD LISTING AS OBSERVED FROM A SIGNED-IN CONSOLE ON 2026-09-05/06, carrying three facts
// the cax41_catalog row above does not: the REGION the rate belongs to, the CURRENCY the console
// quoted, and whether the product could actually be ORDERED.
//
// WHY CURRENCY IS PART OF THE OFFER AND NOT A UNIT TO CONVERT. The row above carries EUR 31.49 per
// month for CAX41, cited from price document GE-D9256. The console quoted USD 48.49 for the same
// SKU. At the ECB reference rate the euro figure converts to about USD 36.60, so the vendor's
// dollar price stands roughly a third ABOVE its own euro price converted. These are therefore two
// DIFFERENT OFFERS made to two different markets, not one magnitude in two units, and an exchange
// rate cannot carry a reader from one to the other. A comparison that FX-converts a vendor's list
// price produces a figure no buyer is ever quoted - which is the specific defect this section was
// written to close, after the model briefly did exactly that.
//
// The consequence for consumers: pick the row whose currency and region match the market being
// served. Do not convert. extdeps.currency.ecb_reference_rate remains correct for euros actually
// SPENT, and is wrong for euros merely QUOTED.

type HetznerCloudRegion
  = Helsinki
  | Nuremberg

fn hetzner_cloud_region_label(r: HetznerCloudRegion) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match r {
    Helsinki => "Helsinki (hel1)",
    Nuremberg => "Nuremberg (nbg1)",
  }
}

// WHETHER A ROW CAN ACTUALLY BE ORDERED IS NOT MODELLED HERE. That reading belongs to whoever
// took it - see gunbc.procurement.hetzner_supply_observation, which joins an account's console
// observation to these rows. DESIGN section 3: observations this repository produces are receipts
// in the observing layer, never properties of the observed upstream.

type HetznerCloudListedRow {
  wire_label: NonEmptyStr
  cpu_architecture: Architecture
  vcpus: HardwareThreadCount
  memory_bytes: ByteSize
  disk_bytes: ByteSize
  hourly: MoneyPerHour
  monthly: MoneyPerMonth
  regions_observed: List<HetznerCloudRegion>
  observation: NonEmptyStr
}

data hetzner_cloud_console_observation: NonEmptyStr = "Operator reading of the signed-in Hetzner Cloud console, 2026-09-05 and 2026-09-06, relayed into session quiet-cat-23. Prices as displayed, excl. VAT. WHAT DETERMINED THE DISPLAY CURRENCY WAS NOT RECORDED - an account billing country, a console selector, or a region default would each produce a dollar figure and they are not the same fact. The rows are therefore carried as USD-quoted-to-this-account rather than as the vendor's universal dollar price, and a consumer serving a differently-billed market must re-read rather than reuse. Memory is recorded in GIBIBYTES following cax41_catalog above, which reads this vendor's GB suffix as binary; the vendor's own suffix is not repeated here so the two rows cannot drift apart on the byte basis."




// THE ROWS. Helsinki and Nuremberg quoted IDENTICAL hourly and monthly figures on every size where
// both were read, so each row carries the list of regions it was observed in rather than being
// duplicated per region - two rows holding one price is the fork DESIGN section 3 forbids, and the
// regions list keeps the observation's real breadth without inviting them to drift.
data hetzner_regions_read: List<HetznerCloudRegion> = [Helsinki, Nuremberg]

